            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a rowing bench, then the Latin for for cargo."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: rudēns, rudentis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rudēns, rudentis — \"a ship's rope, a line\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nautae rudentēs trahunt et vēlum tollunt. The sailors pull the ropes and raise the sail."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, masculine: rudēns, rudentis m. The ropes of the rigging."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of rudēns is not settled: Lewis & Short call it unknown (ancient writers linked it with rudō 'to roar', from the creaking of the ropes), and EtymDB lists it without a parent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
