              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gubernāculum, gubernāculī — \"a rudder, a steering oar\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Gubernātor gubernāculum tenet et nāvem ad portum dūcit. The helmsman holds the rudder and guides the ship to harbour."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, neuter: gubernāculum, gubernāculī n. Also the helm of the state. English govern comes from the verb gubernāre."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gubernāculum is built on gubernō 'to steer' (Lewis & Short), which was borrowed from Greek kybernaō. English govern comes from gubernō through French."
            }
          ]
        },
